-------- mingw/msys2 --------

# Building Snd 26 on Windows (MSYS2 / UCRT64, no GUI)

Tested on Windows 11 ARM64 running the x64 MSYS2 build under emulation,
installer `msys2-x86_64-20260611.exe`. A native x64 machine should behave
the same.

## 1. Packages

In the **UCRT64** shell (not MSYS, not MINGW64):

    pacman -Syu          # close the window, reopen, run it once more
    pacman -S --needed git base-devel mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-toolchain \
      mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-fftw mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-libsndfile \
      mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-portaudio mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-dlfcn

`mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-dlfcn` is the one that is easy to miss. UCRT64 ships
no `dlfcn.h`, and `snd-xen.c` needs it for the `dlopen` bindings.

## 2. Unpack and patch

Build from a path without spaces; the MSYS2 home directory works:

    cd ~
    tar xzf snd.tar.gz
    cd snd-26
#    patch -p0 < snd-mingw.patch # already patched

## 3. Configure and build

    ./configure --without-gui
    sed -i 's/^LIBS = /LIBS = -ldl /' makefile
    make

`configure` already emits `-lwinmm -lwsock32`; only `-ldl` has to be added,
and only because `configure` doesn't yet detect the dlfcn package on mingw.
`s7.c` alone takes several minutes.

## 4. Check

    echo '(display "PING")(newline)' > t.scm
    ./snd -noglob -noinit -l t.scm < /dev/null

Prints `PING`. Note the *relative* filename - see below.

## Open issues

**`-l` rejects absolute paths.** `./snd -l C:/tmp/t.scm` fails with
`can't load C:/tmp/t.scm: Invalid argument`, while `-l t.scm` and `-l ./t.scm`
from that directory both work. The file exists and is readable either way.
This looks like the argument being split on `:` as a path-list separator; on
Windows every absolute path carries a drive colon, so no absolute path can
be passed to `-l` at all. Any tool driving Snd has to chdir first and pass a
bare filename.

**`libc_s7.so`.** Without `-noinit`, startup compiles and dlopens
`libc_s7.so`. On Windows the extension is `.dll`, so the load fails, `*libc*`
stays unbound, and `repl.scm` aborts before the listener is usable.

**Audio untested.** The above covers the non-GUI build used for scripting;
nothing was played.
